#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d23617a-dd03-581c-8b85-ce542f2cb7ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383219} {:answer/id #uuid "66ee4a47-d037-5279-bc33-8f70eabf8d13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383220} {:answer/id #uuid "8d64319a-65c6-54c6-b54d-31b2fecf1c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383221} {:answer/id #uuid "79d91e49-5246-52ce-ac59-216094121dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383222} {:answer/id #uuid "f329ad93-2dbf-5e79-9617-16ebf04d2153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d64319a-65c6-54c6-b54d-31b2fecf1c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383221} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c59e680-ab27-5a9f-a27b-cde02e0e89f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "012179b2-c89e-5745-a680-f7f5cfa0c196" :question/math-academy-id "q-107305" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =3$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6493921c-3b28-567d-b8c6-59d4d06908b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383226} {:answer/id #uuid "014cff4f-292e-5bda-92c0-012532db1031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383227} {:answer/id #uuid "60554852-fcea-57a9-901b-9fa88172485e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383228} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5d6b71-b970-5ec8-bff0-167e4bba82c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383229} {:answer/id #uuid "a843e8ac-955c-5dd7-8ffa-b5521f37db1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6493921c-3b28-567d-b8c6-59d4d06908b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383226} :answer-field/id #uuid "fad4f090-a9e7-579a-8701-46f2afd0ca0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1fd83815-31c4-57cb-8ceb-4b7d6dee8403" :question/math-academy-id "q-108008" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-5$ and $\\lambda =6$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-5)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{9}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32891625-9574-5c20-ad26-45eb81d9ee16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383233} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3544ec-5b8e-5942-bc62-bb29bbb5a498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383234} {:answer/id #uuid "c54f7555-8c95-5c25-854c-0a3f0ab36dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383235} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cc9e44-6859-5b68-a7a9-7968a8266d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383236} {:answer/id #uuid "04663488-c0fe-5751-b914-171cb95b56bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5cc9e44-6859-5b68-a7a9-7968a8266d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383236} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c737afd-53b7-5d46-8c02-c75f15633e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60baa1cc-5bec-5c5e-b7f7-4efe1f7a3de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-36789" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d491b223-3526-5db0-93d1-6b08c1f7b7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to an Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d5d0ded-3265-5619-bf16-41e4ede36d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383240} {:answer/id #uuid "a294065b-c5ec-524d-bb38-66b44adbc4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383241} {:answer/id #uuid "05a327d7-1bc0-58e4-bd27-d8a4e6d43c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383242} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ee4de7-3866-5db3-a9cd-de02f8e09bf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383243} {:answer/id #uuid "f910e73c-fa87-506e-ba75-9aad70290256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05a327d7-1bc0-58e4-bd27-d8a4e6d43c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383242} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d3813a1-03d2-54af-8c13-95d5b91e3efb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66d09f18-29eb-5ec8-ac1c-91dd52fe0ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-80321" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "022c4cb4-d6f6-56a5-a6dc-3adfc5aabda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db1c5ada-5a0a-5063-95fc-28435884e3e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383246} {:answer/id #uuid "7e47c47d-5f99-500a-a4e9-1f25e3e802bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383247} {:answer/id #uuid "0abfab4b-dab0-58b5-a000-0e2dac192e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383248} {:answer/id #uuid "e488e65f-b5f7-5848-a368-c8ad84fc3a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383249} {:answer/id #uuid "f568d7af-2838-5633-b97b-4baee3c39272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e488e65f-b5f7-5848-a368-c8ad84fc3a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383249} :answer-field/id #uuid "27f2d4a8-e7a5-529c-95d3-733cf6b94ace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b43c2e2-c2eb-5c55-afdb-fa448402d1cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-9384" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\lambda =4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-7{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+14{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 14 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 11\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & -7 \\\\ 14 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-14{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+7{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 14 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-14 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
